    "https://hamline.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Faculty_Career_Site/job/St-Paul-Main-Campus/Lecturer---Legal-Studies_R0001271": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Legal Studies",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFounded in 1854, Hamline was the first university in Minnesota and among the first coeducational institutions in the nation. Hamline University provides a world-class education for undergraduate and graduate students. Our faculty, staff, and students cultivate an ethic of civic responsibility, social justice, and inclusive leadership and service. Hamline University is an Equal Opportunity employer. Hamline University does not discriminate on the basis of race; color; religion; creed; sex; national origin; marital status; familial status; status with regard to public assistance; membership or activity in a local human rights commission; disability; age; protected veteran status; or any other status protected by applicable law in its education or employment programs or activities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities. The Division of Social Sciences at Hamline University in St. Paul, Minnesota, invites applicants for a full-time, non-tenure-eligible faculty position as a Lecturer in Legal Studies. C andidates should have a JD, at least three years of experience practicing law, and prior experience teaching undergraduate, graduate, or law students . All legal specialties and practice areas will be considered, although preferred consideration may be given to individuals with experience in housing law, family law, or experience with and understanding of legal technology tools and the legal industry’s experience with Artificial Intelligence tools. The successful candidate will teach 7 courses per academic year within the undergraduate and graduate Legal Studies programs, including the Graduate Paralegal Certificate and the Master in the Study of Law. Courses are in person, hybrid, and online in synchronous and asynchronous modalities. The anticipated start date is January 2027. Required Qualifications: J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school At least 3 years of experience practicing law (in any jurisdiction) Experience working as a paralegal or supervising paralegals in a team environment Teaching experience in a paralegal, legal studies, or law school setting Familiarity with learning management systems (e.g. Canvas, Blackboard, etc.) Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching in a variety of modalities and/or developing online courses in Canvas Interest in emerging technologies, including Artificial Intelligence Background, expertise, or interest in family or housing law or pro bono legal services Hamline University is located in the heart of the culturally vibrant Minneapolis-Saint Paul area.  Legal Studies at Hamline combines a broad interdisciplinary focus with a depth of pre-professional skills needed to work in a variety of law-related fields. Undergraduates enjoy access to multiple opportunities and pathways, including a Law and Society major and a Law School Early Admission program. Graduate programs include a Paralegal Certificate and a Master in the Study of Law. The University serves approximately 1,800 undergraduate students and 800 graduate students. The University’s mission is to create a diverse and collaborative community of learners dedicated to the development of students’ knowledge, values, and skills for successful lives of leadership, scholarship, and service. Hamline University is an affirmative action/equal opportunity employer and educator, and is especially interested in faculty candidates whose work and scholarship will enrich diversity and equality on campus. Candidates will be asked to provide three references during the application process for Hamline to contact for letters of recommendation. All questions marked ‘Required’ need to be answered in order to submit the application. It is important that your application show all of the relevant education and experience you possess. Applications may be rejected if incomplete. If rejected, you must reapply for the position in order to be considered. A resume or curriculum vitae, a cover letter outlining your qualifications for and interest in the position, contact information for 3 current, professional references, as well as copies of transcripts (if required) must be uploaded with each application ."
    },
    "https://hamline.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Faculty_Career_Site/job/St-Paul-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor-Professor-of-Practice-Instructor---Marketing---Management_R0001264": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor/Professor of Practice/Instructor - Marketing & Management",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFounded in 1854, Hamline was the first university in Minnesota and among the first coeducational institutions in the nation. Hamline University provides a world-class education for undergraduate and graduate students. Our faculty, staff, and students cultivate an ethic of civic responsibility, social justice, and inclusive leadership and service. Hamline University is an Equal Opportunity employer. Hamline University does not discriminate on the basis of race; color; religion; creed; sex; national origin; marital status; familial status; status with regard to public assistance; membership or activity in a local human rights commission; disability; age; protected veteran status; or any other status protected by applicable law in its education or employment programs or activities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities. The Hamline School of Business in Saint Paul, Minnesota, seeks applicants for an Assistant Professor or teaching track Professor of Practice or Instructor position in marketing and management. Our vision is to be the place “Where the business, government, and non-profit sectors intersect to educate and collaborate for the common good.” Our faculty, staff, and students cultivate an ethic of civic responsibility, social justice, and inclusive leadership and service. At the undergraduate level, the school offers a Bachelor in Business Administration with concentrations in accounting, business analytics, finance, management, and marketing and a Bachelor of Arts in Economics. At the graduate level, the school offers the following graduate degrees: Master of Business Administration, Master of Management and Leadership, Master of Science in Business Analytics, Master of Management and Leadership, Master of Public Administration, and Ph.D. in Management and Public Service as well as sequential degrees, dual degrees, and a joint MBA/JD degree with the Mitchell Hamline Law School. The School of Business designs its undergraduate and graduate program offerings to meet the needs of students who require the skills and knowledge to succeed in the rapidly changing realities of our local and global communities. The school is dedicated to preparing students to work across sectors to solve the complex issues facing today’s society. Faculty members play a pivotal role in developing new courses and management education certificates as well as ensuring a high level of learning through assessment activities. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES Teaching and curriculum development responsibilities for the tenure-track position includes six courses per year (24 credits across the graduate and undergraduate levels), and the teaching faculty position includes seven courses per year (28 credits across the graduate and undergraduate levels). Potential course content areas include: Marketing Research Digital Marketing intelligence Marketing Automation and AI Foundations of Marketing Foundations of Management Operations Management Project Management Entrepreneurship Strategic Management First-year Seminar Course content matching the expertise of the applicant. - We are seeking candidates who have prior teaching experience (at least one semester) in some of these course content areas. - Preference will be given to candidates with familiarity and interest in AI technologies REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, ABILITIES AND KEY COMPETENCES Candidates must have demonstrable competencies in teaching. Candidates must be committed to the development of skills necessary for advising and mentoring students from culturally diverse backgrounds, and to the promotion and enhancement of diversity, equity, and inclusion in support of academic excellence. These practices include, but are not limited to instructional activities, curriculum content, and contextual framework. Experience teaching online is preferred Preference will be given to candidates with experience working with diverse student populations. MINIMUM EDUCATION/EXPERIENCE Tenure-track Assistant Professor Position Candidates with a completed Ph.D. or D.B.A. in Marketing, Management or Business Administration. ABD candidates will be considered with anticipated completion within the first year of appointment. Teaching Instructor/Professor of Practice Position Candidates with a Master of Business Administration, Master of Management, Master of Marketing or other related Masters degree coupled with substantial experience or a high level of professional achievement in business. APPLICATION PROCESS Please include the following documents with your application:  A letter of interest A curriculum vitae A diversity statement noting how the candidate’s research, teaching, and/or service contributes to diversity, equity, and inclusion A teaching statement that outlines pedagogy and provides evidence of teaching excellence For tenure track applicants, a research statement focusing on past work and future plans Unofficial transcripts of graduate coursework Names and contact information for three references All questions marked ‘Required’ need to be answered in order to submit the application. It is important that your application show all of the relevant education and experience you possess. Applications may be rejected if incomplete. If rejected, you must reapply for the position in order to be considered. A resume or curriculum vitae, a cover letter outlining your qualifications for and interest in the position, contact information for 3 current, professional references, as well as copies of transcripts (if required) must be uploaded with each application ."
    },
    "https://hamline.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Faculty_Career_Site/job/St-Paul-Main-Campus/Professor-of-Practice---Theatre-Design-and-Technology_R0001259": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Theatre Design and Technology",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFounded in 1854, Hamline was the first university in Minnesota and among the first coeducational institutions in the nation. Hamline University provides a world-class education for undergraduate and graduate students. Our faculty, staff, and students cultivate an ethic of civic responsibility, social justice, and inclusive leadership and service. Hamline University is an Equal Opportunity employer. Hamline University does not discriminate on the basis of race; color; religion; creed; sex; national origin; marital status; familial status; status with regard to public assistance; membership or activity in a local human rights commission; disability; age; protected veteran status; or any other status protected by applicable law in its education or employment programs or activities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities. This teaching-track faculty member will support the Department of Performance, Production, and Community (PPC) in its ongoing commitment to academic excellence. Reporting to the PPC Program Director, the Chair of the Division of the Arts, and the Dean of Faculty, this individual will play an integral role in the vibrant and collaborative faculty and staff community at Hamline. The successful candidate will teach courses in theatrical design, manage budgets, lead a team of students to build sets and hang and focus lighting instruments in collaboration with a scene shop supervisor, design for two shows a year, and train and mentor students and student employees. This individual will also coordinate with guest teaching artists. The position collaborates extensively with faculty and staff throughout the Department of Performance, Production, and Community as well as the other programs that make up the Division of the Arts (Music Production, Digital and Studio Arts). The ideal candidate will be: Adept at managing spaces and supervising the technical implementation of scenic and lighting designs. Skilled in the use of technology as it pertains to theatre/performance and instruction of undergraduates Successful at creating culturally responsive environments and classes, using inclusive practices, and planning Highly collaborative, willing and eager to be part of a small department Eager to work and collaborate across disciplinary boundaries with faculty colleagues in the University’s Division of the Arts JOB RESPONSIBILITIES Contribute designs (scenic, lighting, sound, digital projection) for two shows a year Serve as the production manager for season of four shows - managing calendars, budgets, and builds Teach at least one four-credit class a year within the program Maintain the Anne Simley Theatre, making requests for capital budgetary needs when necessary Hire, train and mentor student workers in scene shop/light shop/sound/digital media/box office as well as students interested in pursuing design projects Recruit, support, and retain students from diverse backgrounds Lead a team of students to build sets, hang lights, etc. in collaboration with a scene shop supervisor.  Work with guest artists and guest designers Collaborate with the Program Director in matters pertaining to academic affairs and maintain curricular integrity at the program level Collaborate with other departments across Hamline on innovative approaches to strengthen the programs REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS MFA in theatre design and technology PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS   Honed design aesthetic and avid drive to lead majors and non-majors in developing and realizing innovative, thoughtful, or vivid stage designs  Dedicated interest in production management skills  Interest in developing projection design in practice and pedagogy Investment and willingness to contribute to our commitment to study and practice theatre with and for community All questions marked ‘Required’ need to be answered in order to submit the application. It is important that your application show all of the relevant education and experience you possess. Applications may be rejected if incomplete. If rejected, you must reapply for the position in order to be considered. A resume or curriculum vitae, a cover letter outlining your qualifications for and interest in the position, contact information for 3 current, professional references, as well as copies of transcripts (if required) must be uploaded with each application ."
    },
    "https://hamline.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/Faculty_Career_Site/job/St-Paul-Main-Campus/Assistant-Professor---Music_R0001258-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Music",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFounded in 1854, Hamline was the first university in Minnesota and among the first coeducational institutions in the nation. Hamline University provides a world-class education for undergraduate and graduate students. Our faculty, staff, and students cultivate an ethic of civic responsibility, social justice, and inclusive leadership and service. Hamline University is an Equal Opportunity employer. Hamline University does not discriminate on the basis of race; color; religion; creed; sex; national origin; marital status; familial status; status with regard to public assistance; membership or activity in a local human rights commission; disability; age; protected veteran status; or any other status protected by applicable law in its education or employment programs or activities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to qualified individuals with disabilities. The Hamline University Division of Arts seeks a collaborative musician-educator to join our faculty as a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Music in an innovative curriculum that blends music production and performance. This position is designed for a versatile performer and technologist who is comfortable crossing stylistic borders and blending and bridging genres and traditions. The successful candidate will join a dedicated cohort of faculty focused on a holistic approach to modern music-making. This position requires classroom and studio teaching in areas of history/culture, performance, digital music technologies, and directing an ensemble in their area of expertise. The successful candidate will have a performance specialty - vocal, strings, wood/brasswind, or piano - and experience with both live performance and making music in the box and in the studio.  Primary Responsibilities Classroom Teaching: The successful candidate will contribute their individual strengths and interests to teaching in the core curriculum of the Music Production Major. This may include teaching subjects in contemporary and traditional music history and theory,  composition/song-writing, digital technology, and performance courses. Ensemble Direction : Direct/conduct one or more of the following ensembles: choir, orchestra, band, jazz band, modern or hybrid pop/acoustic/electronic orchestra.  Software and Technology Instruction: Teach courses that integrate assignments using digital music technology (preferably Logic Pro) with practical instruction in gain staging, foundational audio engineering, and basic audio and MIDI editing and programming. Program Support: Participate in curricular development, assessment, recruitment and departmental service as an engaged member of the music faculty team. OPTIONAL Performance Instruction: Provide studio instruction in primary area (vocal, instrumental or composition), focusing on technical development, inclusive repertoire, and applications in traditional and contemporary performance settings. Required Qualifications: Terminal Degree: A PhD, DMA or MFA in Music (Performance, Composition, Music Production, or other relevant specialty) from an accredited college or university or completed ABD by July 31, 2027 (Required at time of appointment) Performance: Active performer at the professional level Music Technology: Competent skills in DAWs (strong preference for Logic Pro) and a foundation in audio hardware (signal flow, interfaces, experience with microphone use as part of live performance and music recording.). Classroom Teaching Experience: A proven record of successful classroom teaching at the high school or collegiate level, minimum 5  years. Collaboration: A demonstrated ability to work effectively with a team, academic or professional, and contribute to a shared vision and mission. Community-oriented:  Professional or pedagogical work connecting with diverse communities outside of academia. Ensemble Leadership: Experience building, maintaining and directing traditional or contemporary ensembles. Professional Portfolio : Evidence of commitment to ongoing creative activity as a performer, sound designer/composer, and/or producer. Interdisciplinary Interest: Experience working across genres, collaborations across disciplines, theater, digital media, creative writing, as well as subjects outside of the arts. Application Materials Cover Letter: Please highlight your experience working within a faculty or professional team, teaching students with a wide range of interests, and connecting your work with the greater community.  Curriculum Vitae Digital Portfolio: Links to recordings or videos that demonstrate: Professional performance (Vocal or Instrumental). Representative production work (recordings, Logic Pro projects or mixed tracks). Optional but encouraged: Video of ensemble conducting, rehearsal leadership, or teaching demo. Statement of Teaching Philosophy. List of Three Professional References. Optional Student Reference Our Program's Guiding Principles: We believe that successful careers in music will increasingly require our students to graduate with a panoply of skills and experiences. Hamline's Major in Music Production develops individual and collaborative skills in live performance, digital sound production, and music composition/design, as well as strong foundations in music history/culture and theory.  The music curriculum is greatly enhanced by Hamline's nationally recognized liberal arts program with abundant opportunities for interdisciplinary experience. We are looking for a colleague who is able and eager to contribute their specific expertise to this growing, multi-faceted program. All questions marked ‘Required’ need to be answered in order to submit the application. It is important that your application show all of the relevant education and experience you possess. Applications may be rejected if incomplete. If rejected, you must reapply for the position in order to be considered. A resume or curriculum vitae, a cover letter outlining your qualifications for and interest in the position, contact information for 3 current, professional references, as well as copies of transcripts (if required) must be uploaded with each application ."
    }
  },
